en.explanation:
## Scenario

A developer using AWS CDK needs to:
1. **Write unit tests** for the IaC (CloudFormation) templates that CDK generates.
2. **Run a validation tool across all constructs** in the CDK app to enforce critical security configurations.

The goal is to do this with **the least development overhead**.

## Correct Answers: B and E

### B — Use the CDK assertions module for unit tests in CI/CD ✅
The CDK ships with a built-in `aws-cdk-lib/assertions` module designed exactly for this use case. The assertions module for the AWS Cloud Development Kit is a set of APIs designed to help you write unit tests against your CDK applications, with a focus on CloudFormation templates. The CDK assertions module provides a robust set of APIs to precisely verify the CloudFormation templates synthesized by your CDK app, and the assertions module is available for every language supported by the CDK.

Using this module instead of writing a custom framework dramatically minimizes development overhead. Running the tests in a CI/CD pipeline on every commit (CodeCommit → CodePipeline) provides automated, continuous validation.

```typescript
import { Template } from 'aws-cdk-lib/assertions';
const template = Template.fromStack(stack);
template.hasResourceProperties('AWS::S3::Bucket', {
  BucketEncryption: { /* ... */ }
});
```

### E — Use the CDK Aspects class to apply security rules across the app ✅
Aspects are the CDK-native mechanism for cross-cutting validations. Aspects are a way to apply an operation to all constructs in a given scope. The aspect could modify the constructs, such as by adding tags, or it could verify something about the state of the constructs, such as making sure that all buckets are encrypted.

When an Aspect calls `Annotations.of(node).addError(...)`, the violation causes synthesis to fail, which is exactly what option E describes. CDK Aspects are ways for developers to align with and check best practices in their infrastructure configurations using the programming language of choice.

```typescript
class BucketEncryptionChecker implements IAspect {
  public visit(node: IConstruct): void {
    if (node instanceof CfnBucket && !node.bucketEncryption) {
      Annotations.of(node).addError('Bucket encryption is not enabled');
    }
  }
}
Aspects.of(app).add(new BucketEncryptionChecker());
```

## Why the other options are wrong

### A — Custom unit tests against `cdk.out` ❌
`cdk.out` contains the synthesized Cloud Assembly artifacts (raw CloudFormation JSON). Writing custom tests that parse those files manually duplicates exactly what the built-in `assertions` module already provides. This adds significant development overhead compared to option B, violating the "LEAST development overhead" requirement.

### C — Use CDK runtime context to set required key-value pairs ❌
`cdk.context.json` (runtime context) is for caching environmental values such as AZ lookups, AMI IDs, and VPC info — it is **not** a validation mechanism. It cannot enforce that "critical security configurations are activated" or fail synthesis on policy violations. This is a misuse of the feature.

### D — Custom script that searches for config strings ❌
A bespoke text/string-search script over CDK source is brittle (it doesn't see auto-generated resources from L2 constructs), language-dependent, and requires writing and maintaining a custom validator. The CDK Aspects framework already exists and is purpose-built for this — option E is far less overhead.

## References
- [Test AWS CDK applications](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cdk/v2/guide/testing.html)
- [aws-cdk-lib.assertions module reference](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cdk/api/v2/docs/aws-cdk-lib.assertions-readme.html)
- [Aspects and the AWS CDK](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cdk/v2/guide/aspects.html)
- [Align with best practices using CDK Aspects (AWS Blog)](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/devops/align-with-best-practices-while-creating-infrastructure-using-cdk-aspects/)
- [Testing CDK Applications in Any Language (AWS Blog)](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/developer/testing-cdk-applications-in-any-language/)

