# Analysis

## Scenario
A static website is hosted in S3 and served via CloudFront with **Origin Access Control (OAC)**. The S3 bucket is private (only CloudFront can access it via the S3 REST endpoint). Requests to a directory path like `/products/` return an error because S3 has no object with that exact key — only `/products/index.html` exists. The developer needs CloudFront to automatically resolve directory requests to the directory's `index.html` **without making the bucket public**.

## Correct Answer: C

Create a **CloudFront Function** attached as a **viewer request** trigger that rewrites the URI by appending `index.html` whenever the request points to a directory (no filename/extension or ends with `/`).

This is exactly the AWS-recommended pattern. CloudFront Functions is the recommended service for lightweight manipulation of items, such as the URI path. The official AWS sample function does this: it intercepts incoming requests to CloudFront and checks that there is a filename and extension. If there isn't a filename and extension, or if the URI ends with a "/", the function appends index.html to the URI.

This works because the S3 REST endpoint does not support redirection to a default index page. CloudFront does allow you to specify a default root object (index.html), but it only works on the root of the website — not subdirectories. The CloudFront Function fills this gap while keeping the bucket private behind OAC.

Sample function (JavaScript, runtime cloudfront-js-1.0):

```javascript
function handler(event) {
    var request = event.request;
    var uri = request.uri;
    if (uri.endsWith('/')) {
        request.uri += 'index.html';
    } else if (!uri.includes('.')) {
        request.uri += '/index.html';
    }
    return request;
}
```

## Why the other options are wrong

### A. Set `index.html` as the CloudFront Default Root Object
The default root object **only applies to the root URL** (e.g., `https://example.com/`). It does **not** apply to subdirectories. There is a feature in CloudFront called the default root object that allows you to specify an index document that applies to the root object only, but not on any subfolders. For example, if you set up index.html as the default root object and a user goes to www.example.com, CloudFront automatically rewrites the request to www.example.com/index.html. But if a user goes to www.example.com/blog, this request is no longer on the root directory, and therefore CloudFront does not rewrite this URL and instead sends it to the origin as is. So `/products/` would still fail.

### B. Enable S3 static website hosting and point CloudFront origin at the S3 website endpoint
While S3 static website hosting natively supports an index document for subdirectories, the S3 **website endpoint is a public HTTP endpoint** and does **not** support OAC. OAC requires the S3 **REST API endpoint** with SigV4 signing. You have to remove the aws_s3_bucket_website_configuration element entirely from your configuration. You won't have any need for it when using CloudFront to publish the site, but more importantly, it won't work together with OAC. You have to disable static website hosting for the S3 bucket. Switching to the website endpoint forces the bucket to be publicly accessible, violating the requirement to not expose the bucket publicly.

### D. Custom error response mapping 404 → `/index.html` with 200
This is the SPA (single-page app) pattern. It would return the **root** `index.html` content (not the directory's specific `index.html`) for every missing path, so `/products/` would return the homepage's content rather than the products page. It also masks legitimate 404 errors by rewriting them all to 200, which is incorrect behavior for a multi-page static site.

## References
- [Implementing Default Directory Indexes in S3-backed CloudFront Origins Using CloudFront Functions](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/implementing-default-directory-indexes-in-amazon-s3-backed-amazon-cloudfront-origins-using-cloudfront-functions/)
- [AWS sample CloudFront Function: url-rewrite-single-page-apps](https://github.com/aws-samples/amazon-cloudfront-functions/blob/main/url-rewrite-single-page-apps/README.md)
- [CloudFront example function: add index.html](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/example-function-add-index.html)

